for people who are homeless or are similarly vulnerable.

255
00:14:24.892 --> 00:14:28.252
Fantastic. Thank you, Allison. We really appreciate both your written and your

256
00:14:28.772 --> 00:14:31.932
oral submission to us today, this afternoon. So thanks for joining us.

257
00:14:32.992 --> 00:14:33.292
Thank you.

258
00:14:34.272 --> 00:14:36.832
Right. Next, we have Madison, who's going to be joining us in person.

259
00:14:42.632 --> 00:14:45.112
Thanks, Madison. You've obviously heard the last couple of submitters.

260
00:14:45.472 --> 00:14:47.612
You've got five minutes. We've read your submission.

261
00:14:48.632 --> 00:14:51.972
But please do leave a little bit of time for questions. Over to you.

262
00:14:52.472 --> 00:14:54.932
Kia ora, everyone. My name's Madison, and I'm a solicitor.

263
00:14:55.272 --> 00:14:58.952
And in my final year of uni, I did an entire law honors dissertation

264
00:14:59.152 --> 00:15:01.872
on the law's impact with homeless people.

265
00:15:02.312 --> 00:15:03.472
That's what I'm relying on today

266
00:15:04.392 --> 00:15:08.372
in my personal capacity. So in my recent submissions, I did six recommendations.

267
00:15:08.452 --> 00:15:09.972
Today, I'm going to be talking about three of them.

268
00:15:10.472 --> 00:15:13.812
The very first one is the bill's stated purpose is to stop

269
00:15:14.072 --> 00:15:16.912
disorderly, threatening, and intimidating behavior.

270
00:15:17.512 --> 00:15:21.292
Rough sleeping and begging are not examples of that, and the bill

271
00:15:21.332 --> 00:15:23.552
doesn't fill a genuine legislative gap.

272
00:15:24.312 --> 00:15:27.862
On a common sense understanding, those behaviors are survival

273
00:15:27.912 --> 00:15:31.772
behaviors carried out in public because the person has nowhere else

274
00:15:31.812 --> 00:15:35.752
to go. And secondly, because the city center is generally safer

275
00:15:35.792 --> 00:15:39.672
with more police and more traffic than other areas of the city.

276
00:15:40.272 --> 00:15:43.532
Section 3 and 5A of the Summary Offences Act already

277
00:15:43.592 --> 00:15:46.172
cover threatening, disorderly, and obstructive behavior.

278
00:15:46.592 --> 00:15:49.712
So do council bylaws right across the country.

279
00:15:49.722 --> 00:15:53.622
If that is the stated purpose of this bill, then it is really doing a

280
00:15:53.632 --> 00:15:57.092
completely unrelated thing. It is stopping the presence of homeless

281
00:15:57.172 --> 00:16:00.492
people in the city. Homeless people are not disorder.

282
00:16:00.992 --> 00:16:04.572
You can see that most clearly in the specific bill's carve outs.

283
00:16:05.052 --> 00:16:07.632
Rough sleeping excludes freedom camping.

284
00:16:07.662 --> 00:16:10.172
Begging excludes charity collectors.

285
00:16:10.212 --> 00:16:12.712
If you're collecting on behalf of a charity, that's fine.

286
00:16:12.732 --> 00:16:16.232
But if you're a homeless person trying to collect your money for dinner, that's not

287
00:16:16.312 --> 00:16:20.092
okay. Just in plain terms, you can be poor,

288
00:16:20.532 --> 00:16:23.492
vulnerable, and homeless, but do it somewhere else.

289
00:16:24.372 --> 00:16:27.972
Just to reiterate, begging and rough sleeping are not disorder.

290
00:16:28.092 --> 00:16:30.372
They are people trying to survive.

291
00:16:30.492 --> 00:16:34.472
I ask the committee to remove from Section 8A of the bill, sub clause

292
00:16:34.652 --> 00:16:35.402
E and F.

293
00:16:36.452 --> 00:16:40.352
The second is the age threshold. Under this bill, a 14-year-old can

294
00:16:40.372 --> 00:16:43.852
be given a move on order for rough sleeping or begging.

295
00:16:43.892 --> 00:16:47.712
A 14-year-old sleeping rough is a child in crisis,

296
00:16:48.992 --> 00:16:52.672
which requires child protection, emergency housing, and social

297
00:16:52.752 --> 00:16:56.532
work. It does not require a fine. Once a child is in the

298
00:16:56.592 --> 00:16:59.592
justice system, it is very hard to get them back out.

299
00:17:00.222 --> 00:17:04.172
The bill in its current form is helping to funnel homeless children

300
00:17:04.212 --> 00:17:05.552
into the criminal justice system.

301
00:17:06.373 --> 00:17:10.252
They're being criminalized for normal behavior and then being fined an

302
00:17:10.292 --> 00:17:13.613
amount that they cannot pay, which may lead to imprisonment.

303
00:17:14.052 --> 00:17:17.572
Which imprisonment on its own has a lifetime effects of

304
00:17:17.613 --> 00:17:21.593
including discriminating children for their status before they've even had a real

305
00:17:21.712 --> 00:17:25.312
chance of working or finding a home that an

306
00:17:25.432 --> 00:17:26.812
adult should be providing to them.

307
00:17:27.652 --> 00:17:31.592
New Zealanders want homeless people, including children, to

308
00:17:31.632 --> 00:17:33.512
be housed, not moved on.

309
00:17:35.352 --> 00:17:38.992
I ask the committee to raise the minimum age to 18 at the minimum.

310
00:17:40.192 --> 00:17:43.992
The third is the cost. A move on fine can run to $2,000.

311
00:17:44.412 --> 00:17:47.132
Someone with no income simply cannot pay that.

312
00:17:47.732 --> 00:17:50.652
Non-payment becomes a warrant, which becomes court time and can end in

313
00:17:50.692 --> 00:17:54.252
imprisonment. We all know that imprisonment costs

314
00:17:55.572 --> 00:17:59.472
about $50,000 a person per year in taxpayer dollars.

315
00:17:59.812 --> 00:18:03.652
That's not an effective use of public money, and no cost assessment of

316
00:18:03.692 --> 00:18:06.052
this bill's enforcement provisions has been published.

317
00:18:06.132 --> 00:18:08.972
I'd ask that one is before this bill goes any further.

318
00:18:09.712 --> 00:18:11.512
So my submission is really narrow.

319
00:18:11.572 --> 00:18:15.092
You need to be removing rough sleeping and begging as grounds for a move on order.

320
00:18:15.382 --> 00:18:18.920
And secondly The minimum age should be raised to 18 at the

321
00:18:18.980 --> 00:18:22.910
least. Once those provisions are removed, it becomes illogical to have

322
00:18:22.940 --> 00:18:26.700
this bill in place at all. We've already got Summary Offences Act,

323
00:18:26.760 --> 00:18:28.620
which covers disorderly and offensive behavior.

324
00:18:30.240 --> 00:18:31.120
Welcome any questions.

325
00:18:31.420 --> 00:18:33.599
Thank you, Madison. We've got a question from Camilla Belich.

326
00:18:34.000 --> 00:18:37.960
Thanks for your submission, and amazing that you've done research in this area.

327
00:18:37.990 --> 00:18:40.440
It's a really well-written submission. I'm really put.

328
00:18:41.180 --> 00:18:45.020
I just had a question around the age threshold, which I agree

329
00:18:45.080 --> 00:18:48.480
with you on that point. But we've had some people come to the committee and say,

330
00:18:48.590 --> 00:18:52.400
"Well, the criminal age of responsibility is 14." Can you

331
00:18:52.560 --> 00:18:56.320
just maybe expand a bit on what you've put in your written submission around-

332
00:18:56.540 --> 00:18:56.820
Yeah

333
00:18:56.840 --> 00:18:56.840
...

334
00:18:57.720 --> 00:19:00.800
kind of the practicalities of how

335
00:19:01.320 --> 00:19:05.270
removing that age from 14 to 18 would

336
00:19:05.380 --> 00:19:05.470
work?

337
00:19:05.470 --> 00:19:05.600
Yeah.

338
00:19:06.690 --> 00:19:10.609
The main reason why I've made the age 18 is because if you've got

339
00:19:10.860 --> 00:19:14.780
a child who's homeless, any age under 18

340
00:19:14.860 --> 00:19:18.710
is you're really expecting a parent to be there and helping them get housed.

341
00:19:19.340 --> 00:19:23.270
That's evidently not happening if they're age 14 to 18.

342
00:19:24.320 --> 00:19:28.140
So at minimum, we should be raising it to 18, just so

343
00:19:28.200 --> 00:19:32.100
that we're putting those people who are disadvantaged on the same position as

344
00:19:32.160 --> 00:19:35.960
an ordinary New Zealander who's got parents who are helping them get housed.

345
00:19:38.080 --> 00:19:39.400
There's so many other reasons for that, too.

346
00:19:39.620 --> 00:19:40.080
It's just like-

347
00:19:40.090 --> 00:19:41.990
Yeah. That's a really good point, I think, that we haven't heard before as well,

348
00:19:42.060 --> 00:19:46.000
that if they are on the street, then effectively that's an indication that there

349
00:19:46.040 --> 00:19:47.920
either isn't a parent involved or that that-

350
00:19:47.990 --> 00:19:47.990
Yeah

351
00:19:48.000 --> 00:19:50.180
... parental responsibility is not being exercised, I think.

352
00:19:50.220 --> 00:19:50.480
Yeah.

353
00:19:51.740 --> 00:19:52.160
Anything else?

354
00:19:53.180 --> 00:19:56.080
Thank you, Madison. That's the end of our time with you, but we really appreciate

355
00:19:56.120 --> 00:19:57.680
your hard work and your submission today.

356
00:19:57.940 --> 00:19:58.000
Thank you.

357
00:19:58.040 --> 00:20:00.700
Thanks. Thank you very much. Our next submitter is Kyle.

358
00:20:10.180 --> 00:20:12.320
Hey, Kyle. Thank you for joining us this afternoon.

359
00:20:12.440 --> 00:20:15.260
We've read your written submission, and we've got you down for five minutes.

360
00:20:15.640 --> 00:20:16.120
Over to you.

361
00:20:20.680 --> 00:20:24.660
Thank you. Tēnā koutou katoa. As you've read in my submission, my name is Kyle

