00:56:25.936 --> 00:56:28.156
There's other problems with the proposal.

993
00:56:28.416 --> 00:56:29.316
First of all, the

994
00:56:30.336 --> 00:56:34.236
power for a police officer to arrest a young person is pretty heavily

995
00:56:34.256 --> 00:56:37.916
prescribed. There's all sorts of hoops that they have to jump

996
00:56:37.936 --> 00:56:38.356
through,

997
00:56:39.396 --> 00:56:41.636
and the process is not simple.

998
00:56:42.996 --> 00:56:46.516
So I think it's going to be pretty difficult for a police

999
00:56:46.576 --> 00:56:47.436
officer to

1000
00:56:48.436 --> 00:56:52.286
arrest a young person who may be in breach of a move on order,

1001
00:56:52.576 --> 00:56:53.556
even if they do breach it.

1002
00:56:54.876 --> 00:56:58.636
And to cap things off, there are two principles in the Oranga Tamariki Act,

1003
00:56:58.856 --> 00:57:01.776
which to me suggests that the chances of a

1004
00:57:03.716 --> 00:57:06.696
breach case ever making court is very unlikely.

1005
00:57:07.976 --> 00:57:11.676
First of all, unless the public interest requires otherwise, criminal

1006
00:57:11.696 --> 00:57:15.416
procedures should not be instituted against a young person

1007
00:57:16.256 --> 00:57:18.976
if there's an alternative means of dealing with the matter.

1008
00:57:19.616 --> 00:57:20.326
Hear, hear.

1009
00:57:20.376 --> 00:57:24.256
And arresting a young person only because they're homeless or being

1010
00:57:24.616 --> 00:57:26.836
a slight nuisance should not qualify, in my view.

1011
00:57:28.156 --> 00:57:31.926
And secondly, criminal proceedings should not

1012
00:57:32.116 --> 00:57:35.876
be instituted against a child or young person in order to give

1013
00:57:35.916 --> 00:57:38.576
assistance or services needed to advance their wellbeing.

1014
00:57:39.736 --> 00:57:42.836
Those two principles are contained in Section 208 of the Act.

1015
00:57:44.296 --> 00:57:48.116
So in summary, I would suggest that if a police officer finds a

1016
00:57:48.156 --> 00:57:52.096
young person who's homeless, they've already got power to

1017
00:57:52.156 --> 00:57:52.576
take them

1018
00:57:53.536 --> 00:57:57.136
and place them in the care of Oranga Tamariki if a parental home is not available.

1019
00:57:57.756 --> 00:58:00.096
That's the power that they can and should exercise,

1020
00:58:00.976 --> 00:58:04.776
and it's extraordinarily unlikely that an arrest would ever be

1021
00:58:04.876 --> 00:58:08.696
justified or charges against a young person end up in court

1022
00:58:08.816 --> 00:58:10.956
if they did breach this bill, if it's passed.

1023
00:58:12.256 --> 00:58:16.116
So my urging to you is to recommend that the bill be amended

1024
00:58:16.196 --> 00:58:19.576
so it doesn't apply to young people aged between 14 and 18.

1025
00:58:21.196 --> 00:58:21.286
Kia ora.

1026
00:58:21.336 --> 00:58:23.896
Thank you, Greg, and thank you for your written submission as well.

1027
00:58:24.146 --> 00:58:25.796
Doesn't appear that we have any questions for-

1028
00:58:26.256 --> 00:58:26.465
I have a question.

1029
00:58:26.465 --> 00:58:26.956
Oh, Tamatha Paul.

1030
00:58:27.576 --> 00:58:29.256
Thanks, Greg, for your submission.

1031
00:58:30.156 --> 00:58:33.876
One thing that I hear when people are talking about young people and the

1032
00:58:33.936 --> 00:58:37.636
effect that this bill has on them is, where are their parents?

1033
00:58:38.006 --> 00:58:41.876
And I wondered, with your decades of experience advocating for young people, if you

1034
00:58:41.956 --> 00:58:44.916
might want to enlighten the committee about where these kids' parents

1035
00:58:45.036 --> 00:58:45.956
are.

1036
00:58:47.976 --> 00:58:51.726
Far too many of them have families that are devastated by poverty,

1037
00:58:52.496 --> 00:58:56.016
and they're just coping by themselves, or they've got a split

1038
00:58:56.116 --> 00:59:00.036
family, or they've got family circumstances which

1039
00:59:00.116 --> 00:59:02.596
aren't ideal, or it's not even safe for them to go back home.

1040
00:59:03.276 --> 00:59:06.256
So, that just doesn't happen, and then the

1041
00:59:07.256 --> 00:59:11.236
state should stand up and give them care and assistance, and

1042
00:59:11.716 --> 00:59:13.076
there are means of doing that already.

1043
00:59:14.036 --> 00:59:15.056
So to

1044
00:59:16.116 --> 00:59:19.516
say to a police officer that they should tell this young person in these

1045
00:59:19.536 --> 00:59:21.276
circumstances that they've got to go elsewhere,

1046
00:59:22.096 --> 00:59:25.796
to me is the wrong response. It shouldn't be what they should do.

1047
00:59:25.856 --> 00:59:28.356
There should be a therapeutic approach instead.

1048
00:59:29.776 --> 00:59:30.216
Thank you.

1049
00:59:30.956 --> 00:59:33.886
Thank you very much, Greg, and thank you for joining us this afternoon and for your

1050
00:59:33.896 --> 00:59:36.256
written submission as well.

1051
00:59:36.296 --> 00:59:37.336
Kia ora.

1052
00:59:37.376 --> 00:59:40.896
All right. Our next submitter is Nikki, who's also going to be joining us on Zoom.

1053
01:00:00.396 --> 01:00:00.776
Kia ora.

1054
01:00:01.756 --> 01:00:03.796
Kia ora, Nikki. Thank you for joining us this afternoon.

1055
01:00:04.116 --> 01:00:06.916
We've read your written submission, and we've got you down for five minutes.

1056
01:00:07.276 --> 01:00:07.676
Over to you.

1057
01:00:08.496 --> 01:00:12.416
Kia ora. Tēnā koutou katoa. Ko Ingarangi te whakapaparanga

1058
01:00:12.456 --> 01:00:16.116
mai, engari ko Aotearoa te whenua tupu,

1059
01:00:16.596 --> 01:00:20.516
ko Ngā Motu te kāinga, ko Nikki toku ingoa. Tēnā tātou katoa.

1060
01:00:21.336 --> 01:00:24.876
Thank you to the committee for the opportunity to speak to my submission today.

1061
01:00:25.676 --> 01:00:29.476
I'm here today to oppose this bill in its entirety, and I'll use my

1062
01:00:29.556 --> 01:00:30.736
time to explain why.

1063
01:00:31.756 --> 01:00:34.956
I speak with previous experience of supporting vulnerable people through the

1064
01:00:34.976 --> 01:00:38.516
healthcare systems in both Aotearoa and in the UK.

1065
01:00:39.076 --> 01:00:42.486
Through those experiences, I've seen firsthand what poverty and

1066
01:00:42.576 --> 01:00:44.956
homelessness do to a person's body and mind.

1067
01:00:45.916 --> 01:00:49.636
The chronic health issues, the mental health crises that spiral because there's

1068
01:00:49.676 --> 01:00:53.455
nowhere safe to rest. The slow erosion of dignity that comes from

1069
01:00:53.516 --> 01:00:57.256
being told again and again that you can't be in the place where you are or where

1070
01:00:57.276 --> 01:00:58.176
you found yourself.

1071
01:00:59.476 --> 01:01:01.536
The bill says that it will do three things.

1072
01:01:01.596 --> 01:01:05.376
It'll equip police to deal with public disorder, it will increase the public sense

1073
01:01:05.416 --> 01:01:08.996
of safety, and support urban centers and local economies.

1074
01:01:09.296 --> 01:01:12.536
I don't believe it will do any of these things effectively, and I'll talk through

1075
01:01:12.576 --> 01:01:12.836
why

1076
01:01:14.188 --> 01:01:17.838
First, moving people on does not resolve disorder, it relocates

1077
01:01:17.868 --> 01:01:21.658
it. If someone is sleeping rough outside a shop and police move them

1078
01:01:21.728 --> 01:01:25.548
on, they do not disappear. They walk to the next street, the next doorway, the next

1079
01:01:25.608 --> 01:01:29.328
town. The underlying reasons that they were there in the first place, no

1080
01:01:29.408 --> 01:01:33.368
housing, no income, sometimes no support network at all, travel with

1081
01:01:33.428 --> 01:01:35.768
them. We're not solving a problem here.

1082
01:01:35.828 --> 01:01:39.428
This is just creating costs for the taxpayer and the further

1083
01:01:39.468 --> 01:01:42.568
destabilization of people who are already in crisis.

1084
01:01:43.548 --> 01:01:47.448
A great many of the people that this bill will actually be used against are not

1085
01:01:47.488 --> 01:01:50.028
disorderly in any way. They're unsupported.

1086
01:01:50.688 --> 01:01:54.288
When a person in psychological distress is sleeping rough or behaving

1087
01:01:54.328 --> 01:01:57.648
erratically in public, a move on order does not connect them to care.

1088
01:01:58.208 --> 01:02:01.608
It simply pushes them out of sight, with a constable, not a clinician,

1089
01:02:02.088 --> 01:02:05.028
as the first and only response. That is not treatment.

1090
01:02:05.628 --> 01:02:09.588
It's displacement dressed up as enforcement, and it will fall

1091
01:02:09.648 --> 01:02:13.068
hardest on the people the mental health system has already failed to reach.

1092
01:02:14.148 --> 01:02:18.008
Second, on public safety, a person who's just been fined or arrested for standing

1093
01:02:18.068 --> 01:02:22.018
still in the wrong place is not safer, and neither is anyone around them.

1094
01:02:22.028 --> 01:02:24.928
What actually increases community safety is stability.

1095
01:02:25.138 --> 01:02:28.248
People housed, people supported and connected to services.

1096
01:02:28.868 --> 01:02:32.208
Criminalizing the visible symptoms of poverty doesn't make it go away.

1097
01:02:32.248 --> 01:02:35.248
It simply makes a punishable offense to be poor in public.

1098
01:02:36.188 --> 01:02:40.158
Third, on supporting urban centers and economies, retailers and city communities

1099
01:02:40.308 --> 01:02:43.248
are struggling with disorder, and I don't dismiss that concern.

1100
01:02:43.868 --> 01:02:46.828
But businesses don't want the problem just shuffled around the corner.

1101
01:02:46.868 --> 01:02:50.568
They want it solved, and that means investment in outreach, in

1102
01:02:50.608 --> 01:02:53.598
emergency and transitional housing, in addiction and mental health

1103
01:02:53.628 --> 01:02:57.588
services. That's what rebuilds confidence in town centers.

1104
01:02:57.608 --> 01:03:01.048
A move on order is not a solution. It's a postponement with a fine

1105
01:03:01.108 --> 01:03:01.678
attached.

1106
01:03:02.668 --> 01:03:06.268
I also want to just raise with the committee something they should sit with, that

1107
01:03:06.328 --> 01:03:09.508
homelessness in this country is growing faster than our population.

1108
01:03:10.068 --> 01:03:13.688
That's the emergency. Every year, this bill would mean that more people who've done

1109
01:03:13.728 --> 01:03:17.608
nothing wrong except run out of options are pushed into contact with

1110
01:03:17.648 --> 01:03:21.008
the justice system for the simple act of existing in a public space.

1111
01:03:22.007 --> 01:03:25.628
That pressure then lands on a corrections system that's already stretched to its

1112
01:03:25.668 --> 01:03:25.988
limit.

1113
01:03:27.148 --> 01:03:31.048
Finally, I'd ask the committee to consider the state of public trust in police

1114
01:03:31.128 --> 01:03:34.508
right now. Confidence has been shaken by concerns about

1115
01:03:34.608 --> 01:03:37.848
accountability and judgment in the use of existing powers.

1116
01:03:38.388 --> 01:03:42.288
Handing over broader powers to move people on at a moment when

1117
01:03:42.388 --> 01:03:46.238
that trust is fragile does not strengthen the relationship between

1118
01:03:46.248 --> 01:03:48.508
police and the communities they serve.

1119
01:03:48.568 --> 01:03:51.378
Those vulnerable communities, most of all.

1120
01:03:51.708 --> 01:03:55.618
I recognize the committee is genuinely trying to respond to real concerns from

1121
01:03:55.648 --> 01:03:59.548
real communities, but I would ask you to redirect that energy toward

1122
01:03:59.588 --> 01:04:03.357
housing, toward health, and toward addiction services, and

1123
01:04:03.408 --> 01:04:06.948
restoring confidence in policing rather than towards this

1124
01:04:07.008 --> 01:04:10.728
legislation that punishes people for the outcomes of

1125
01:04:10.828 --> 01:04:12.288
policy failures elsewhere.

1126
01:04:13.278 --> 01:04:16.908
So for these reasons, and consistent with this committee's duties under Te

1127
01:04:16.968 --> 01:04:20.888
Tiriti o Waitangi to uphold the health, the safety, and the well-being

1128
01:04:20.928 --> 01:04:24.788
of all the communities in Aotearoa, I respectfully ask that

1129
01:04:24.908 --> 01:04:27.388
you reject this bill. Tena koutou katoa.

1130
01:04:28.388 --> 01:04:31.768
Kia ora, Nicky. Thank you very much for joining us this afternoon and for your

1131
01:04:31.788 --> 01:04:34.088
written submission as well. We do really appreciate it.

1132
01:04:34.128 --> 01:04:34.808
Thank you.

1133
01:04:37.168 --> 01:04:40.868
Okay, our next submitter is going to be Still Waters Community Wellington, and

1134
01:04:40.888 --> 01:04:42.708
we've got Sasha, who's going to be presenting.

1135
01:04:44.528 --> 01:04:48.158
I've got two other members of the community who have joined me at the table for

1136
01:04:48.158 --> 01:04:48.168
today.

1137
01:04:48.188 --> 01:04:49.968
Fantastic. If you just want to introduce them to us.

1138
01:04:50.408 --> 01:04:50.488
So

1139
01:04:51.137 --> 01:04:52.508
Hi, I'm Larissa Ward. I'm-

1140
01:04:52.928 --> 01:04:53.468
Hey, Larissa.

1141
01:04:53.508 --> 01:04:53.808
Larissa.

1142
01:04:56.868 --> 01:04:57.908
You come here. And this is Tania.

1143
01:04:59.468 --> 01:04:59.748
Hi.

1144
01:05:00.148 --> 01:05:00.488
Kia ora.

1145
01:05:02.688 --> 01:05:04.888
Fantastic. Sasha, we've got you down for 10 minutes.

1146
01:05:05.028 --> 01:05:07.388
We've read your written submission, but over to you.

1147
01:05:07.768 --> 01:05:11.008
Awesome. Thank you. So tena koutou katoa.

1148
01:05:11.608 --> 01:05:14.908
Thank you for the opportunity to speak today. My name's Sasha Green.

1149
01:05:15.448 --> 01:05:19.208
I'm here with members of the Still Waters community, there are others

1150
01:05:19.808 --> 01:05:20.748
in the seats,

1151
01:05:21.828 --> 01:05:25.708
to express our strong opposition to the Summary Offences Orders

1152
01:05:25.848 --> 01:05:26.528
Amendment Bill.

1153
01:05:27.768 --> 01:05:30.888
Still Waters is a Christian community in inner city Wellington.

1154
01:05:31.368 --> 01:05:35.248
We've been around for 35 years, based in Cuba Street and

1155
01:05:35.288 --> 01:05:38.808
Willis Street. We aim to welcome people, give space for

1156
01:05:38.848 --> 01:05:42.348
people to belong and participate and care for each

1157
01:05:42.428 --> 01:05:46.148
other. We're a place where people who may have been excluded or

1158
01:05:46.208 --> 01:05:49.048
made to feel unwelcome elsewhere

1159
01:05:49.788 --> 01:05:53.428
to come along and contribute and have their dignity and mana

1160
01:05:53.488 --> 01:05:57.408
upheld. Each week, we gather for a community meal

1161
01:05:57.568 --> 01:05:59.868
and our inclusive expression of church.

1162
01:06:00.328 --> 01:06:04.268
Throughout the week, we offer practical support, advocacy, food, friendship,

1163
01:06:04.308 --> 01:06:08.118
and connection. Many people who are part of our community have

1164
01:06:08.148 --> 01:06:12.008
experienced or are currently experiencing homelessness, housing

1165
01:06:12.028 --> 01:06:15.728
insecurity, poverty, addiction, mental distress, and social

1166
01:06:15.848 --> 01:06:19.548
isolation. Those experiences shape what we want to say

1167
01:06:19.668 --> 01:06:20.508
today.

1168
01:06:21.588 --> 01:06:23.728
And we're a community of,

1169
01:06:24.988 --> 01:06:28.288
at any time, kind of 50 plus people.

1170
01:06:28.408 --> 01:06:32.038
But over those 35 years, hundreds and hundreds of people have

1171
01:06:32.108 --> 01:06:35.708
been part of Still Waters and impacted by it.

1172
01:06:35.748 --> 01:06:39.468
From our experience, people who are rough sleeping or begging are not the

1173
01:06:39.548 --> 01:06:40.768
problem to be solved.

1174
01:06:41.648 --> 01:06:44.928
Their circumstances reflect the consequences of problems our

1175
01:06:44.968 --> 01:06:48.648
society has failed to solve. This bill treats the

1176
01:06:48.708 --> 01:06:52.678
visible effects of those failures as the issue rather than addressing the

1177
01:06:52.788 --> 01:06:53.348
causes.

1178
01:06:54.248 --> 01:06:56.348
Move on orders do not create housing.

1179
01:06:56.708 --> 01:07:00.508
They do not provide mental health support, addiction treatment, or income.

1180
01:07:01.048 --> 01:07:04.748
They propose to move people on without it being clear where

1181
01:07:04.788 --> 01:07:08.688
those people can go and risk criminalizing poverty

1182
01:07:09.128 --> 01:07:11.428
and pulling people into the justice system.

1183
01:07:12.608 --> 01:07:16.588
When you've got nowhere to go and you're being told to move on, that doesn't

1184
01:07:16.648 --> 01:07:17.328
solve anything

1185
01:07:18.200 --> 01:07:20.540
You don't suddenly have somewhere safe to sleep.

1186
01:07:21.200 --> 01:07:23.300
And just like this floor, you're not welcome.

1187
01:07:25.060 --> 01:07:29.020
Our opposition to the bill is not about saying, "Do nothing." It is

1188
01:07:29.080 --> 01:07:33.060
about asking Parliament to invest in things that make a real

1189
01:07:33.100 --> 01:07:33.720
difference.

1190
01:07:34.680 --> 01:07:38.440
So we are asking you to invest in housing so people have places to

1191
01:07:38.460 --> 01:07:42.020
go. We want to increase the mental health,

1192
01:07:42.140 --> 01:07:45.360
addiction, and outreach services that people need.

1193
01:07:46.040 --> 01:07:49.980
And to tell you that there is a better way to

1194
01:07:50.020 --> 01:07:53.980
support the community-based responses where people can belong,

1195
01:07:54.100 --> 01:07:57.800
be treated with dignity, and get support, and an upper hand

1196
01:07:57.900 --> 01:08:00.160
so they can get out of these circumstances.

1197
01:08:00.240 --> 01:08:04.210
Because a lot of the time, people don't get enough money to get up out

1198
01:08:04.220 --> 01:08:07.800
of these circumstances. What you want everybody to eventually be

1199
01:08:08.540 --> 01:08:11.820
productive people of the community. Well, they just need the extra support.

1200
01:08:11.850 --> 01:08:15.020
And I've done research, like Department of Conservation, there's lots of

1201
01:08:15.140 --> 01:08:18.200
spare sections of land where yous could put

1202
01:08:18.480 --> 01:08:19.500
prefabricated

1203
01:08:20.700 --> 01:08:24.300
cabins on there that would be way more cheaper than buying hotels,

1204
01:08:24.800 --> 01:08:28.780
putting people in prison for 50,000 a pop just to get them out and

1205
01:08:28.820 --> 01:08:29.440
in the same

1206
01:08:30.620 --> 01:08:34.141
predicament. They need a lot of support. I was homeless.

1207
01:08:34.700 --> 01:08:38.601
I was mental. I was really sick. And just with the right people stepping in,

1208
01:08:39.061 --> 01:08:42.940
helping me, I got to here. And there is a lot of hope

1209
01:08:43.000 --> 01:08:43.250
for them.

1210
01:08:44.221 --> 01:08:47.950
People necessarily don't want to be stuck there with no education or not

1211
01:08:47.980 --> 01:08:51.820
believing in themselves. But we have this thing in society where a lot of

1212
01:08:51.860 --> 01:08:55.639
them are trauma patients or with mental illness, and if they can be

1213
01:08:55.760 --> 01:08:59.641
just dealt with the right way, they will be productive people.

1214
01:08:59.740 --> 01:09:03.601
Now I'm looking for work, and now I'm housed, and now I'm

1215
01:09:03.681 --> 01:09:07.200
on medication, and I'm in a lot better situation than I

1216
01:09:07.260 --> 01:09:11.181
was. And it took people like this community, and a

1217
01:09:11.221 --> 01:09:14.580
lot of people like yous, too. So thank you for listening to us.

1218
01:09:15.351 --> 01:09:18.790
So at Still Waters, we've done a lot of learning together about

1219
01:09:18.840 --> 01:09:22.721
creating a more caring society. We want to share some of

1220
01:09:22.780 --> 01:09:26.460
this to encourage you to think about a different approach than is being

1221
01:09:26.540 --> 01:09:29.580
proposed in the Move on Orders Bill. Annya.

1222
01:09:29.740 --> 01:09:32.700
Communities are stronger when we treat people with compassion,

1223
01:09:33.500 --> 01:09:37.160
kindness, and care. Kai matters. When people can feed

1224
01:09:37.200 --> 01:09:41.000
themselves and their tamariki, they can make better

1225
01:09:41.061 --> 01:09:44.360
decisions. When people are given the chance to participate and

1226
01:09:44.440 --> 01:09:47.721
contribute to society, they can belong.

1227
